From d6204a4e3ffc2d88de03ac445392b63c010e695c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius Gehrmann <marius@gehrmann.solutions>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:34:43 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 19/22] drm: apple: Report the J613 panel disconnected while
 the lid is closed

Once the J613 internal DCP was told the lid is closed (A475(2)), its
firmware takes the pipe for a second external display at every panel
power-on and swallows each panel frame ("swap_submit_dcp: swallowed swap
... as timings are not enabled for second external dispay on internal
pipe"). A swallowed frame never completes: five seconds later the swap
call times out and the DCP is gone until reboot, panel and Type-C route
with it. Desktops light the panel with the lid closed in two common
cases: Omarchy's clamshell handling enables the internal output whenever
no external display is active, so unplugging the last external display
with the lid closed did it, and so did booting with the lid closed
(Hyprland enables every connected output at start). Both lost the DCP in
testing on J613.

Treat a closed lid as a panel that is not there, as macOS does:

- While the lid is closed the panel's connector reads disconnected, from
  boot on if the lid is closed then (the clamshell_panel_away state the
  previous patch used while a Type-C display had the pipe, now for the
  whole time the lid is closed). Compositors switch its CRTC off and
  leave it off; with no display at all they lock and suspend without
  lighting it.
- Closing the lid powers the panel off at once (A472(0)), whether its
  CRTC is on or not, after a black frame while IOMFB still has the lid
  open. From A475(2) on no frame goes to the panel; a frame that raced
  with the lid is dropped under the session lock, not sent.
- A CRTC switched on with the lid closed leaves the panel off and gets
  no frames (one warning), instead of powering it on into a firmware
  that would swallow its frames. When the lid opens, IOMFB is told first
  (A475(1)) and then a panel whose CRTC is on is powered on, from the lid
  change or from the next mode set, whichever comes first.
- The pipe is free for a Type-C display only once the panel's CRTC is off
  too, so that a display taking the pipe never shares its CRTC with a
  panel the compositor still drives.

When the lid opens with no Type-C display on the pipe, the panel's
connector reads connected again at once; after a Type-C display let go
of the pipe, once the compositor switched the CRTC off, or two seconds
later, as before.

